 Advanced Docker Security
Objectives
Design and implement a hardened Docker daemon configuration that enforces user namespace remapping, resource limits, and a custom seccomp profile from scratch
Build a minimal, patched container image using multi-stage builds and validate its attack surface through automated vulnerability scanning
Construct an end-to-end security scanning pipeline that evaluates images for CVEs, secrets, and Dockerfile misconfigurations and produces a structured report
Prerequisites
Comfort with Linux command-line operations including file editing, process management, and reading man pages
Familiarity with Linux user and group management, file permissions, and basic networking concepts
Lab Environment
You will work on a dedicated AWS EC2 Ubuntu instance provided by Al Nafi. The instance has a base Ubuntu installation; you will install all required tools in Task 1.

Task 1: Install and Verify the Security Toolchain
Requirement 1
Install Docker Engine, Trivy, Hadolint, and Dockle on the bare Ubuntu instance. Each tool must be installed from its official source. After installation, confirm that the Docker daemon is active, that each CLI tool responds to a version or help flag, and that a basic docker run hello-world completes without error.

# Official Docker installation guide (use this if any step below fails):
# https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/ubuntu/

sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y ca-certificates curl gnupg lsb-release

sudo install -m 0755 -d /etc/apt/keyrings
curl -fsSL https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu/gpg \
  | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg
sudo chmod a+r /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg

sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list <<EOF
deb [arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture) signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg] https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu $(lsb_release -cs) stable
EOF

sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io
sudo systemctl enable --now docker
sudo usermod -aG docker "$USER"
newgrp docker
docker run hello-world
